# ==== T-FMT-C · a trailing comment on a CLAUSE or ARM header stays on the header
#
# `else:  # note` used to drop its trailing comment into the branch body, where
# `format_block_stmts` re-emitted it as a LEADING comment on the branch's first
# statement — so a note about the `else` came out reading as a note about
# whatever happened to come first inside it. The misattribution class T-FMT-A
# retired, surviving at the sites nobody censused.
#
# It survived because of an ASYMMETRY, not an oversight in the abstract: `elif`
# fired a header hook and so did `case`, so those two positions were right; only
# `else` had none.
#
# ── AXIS 1 · CLAUSE POSITION, indented body (9 cells) ─────────────────────────
#   STATEMENT level (6): `if`-`else` · statement-match `else` · `for` `else` ·
#     `while` `else` · `meta match` `else` · expression-match `else`.
#   ITEM level (3): the `meta if` header itself, its `elif`, its `else` — same
#     class one layer up, where the nested-item loop re-emitted a header's
#     comment as a LEADING comment on the branch's first DEFINITION.
#
#   Seven of the nine go through ONE producer, `Formatter::emit_else_header`
#   (header + hook + newline). The two that do not are named rather than
#   glossed: the `meta if` and `elif` ITEM headers carry a condition, so they
#   write their own header text and call the hook directly — and the
#   expression-match `else` reaches its body through `format_arm_body`, which
#   owns hook placement for every ARM-shaped position (axis 2). The site counts
#   are pinned by `formatter_sibling_loops_hook_pairing` in tests/lints.rs, not
#   restated here where they would rot.
#
#   The item-level `else` anchors on `MetaIf.else_keyword_span`, WRITTEN by the
#   parser. It used to walk backwards from the first item to find the colon;
#   that walk is gone.
#
# ── AXIS 2 · ARM POSITION × BODY SHAPE (8 cells, COMPLETE) ────────────────────
#   `format_arm_body` has FOUR call sites — `format_match_arm` (a `case` arm),
#   the expression-match `else`, `catch`, and `rethrow` — and each hands over
#   its own anchor. Every one of the four is sampled against both body shapes
#   that put the comment somewhere non-obvious:
#
#                      author `do:`        inline expression
#     case             do x case           inline x case
#     else             do x else           inline x else
#     catch            do x catch          inline x catch
#     rethrow          do x rethrow        inline x rethrow
#
#   The third body shape — an INDENTED suite — is axis 1 above; a `case` or
#   `catch` with an indented body is the same code path as the `else` cells.
#
#   WHY BOTH SHAPES ARE HERE. An author `do:` belongs to the HEADER's line, so
#   the comment is emitted after ` do:`; firing the hook before it spells
#   `else:  # note do:` and eats the keyword, which is ACCEPT/REJECT-relevant
#   (see the note on `E_MoveInOperandPosition` at `format_arm_body`). An INLINE
#   body SHARES the header's line, so its comment is CLAIMED at the header and
#   emitted after the body — emitting at the colon would put it ahead of the
#   value it heads and the output would not re-parse.
#
#   WHY ONE CELL PER CALL SITE and not one per producer: the anchor is supplied
#   PER SITE, and a wrong per-site anchor is exactly the defect that opened this
#   work — the expression-match `else` and `format_match_arm` already shared a
#   producer and still disagreed, because each re-derived the decision. Two
#   earlier versions of this file shipped an arm with NO cell at all (first the
#   author-`do:` shape, then `rethrow`), and in both cases every gate stayed
#   green while the mechanism was disabled.
#
# ── RED, per cell (break-and-restore, re-recorded 2026-08-11) ─────────────────
#   AXIS 1: every cell moved its comment down one line and one indent level into
#     the branch body.
#   AXIS 2, `do:` row: worse, and the costume differed by position — the `case`
#     one re-emitted the comment as a TRAILING comment on the branch's LAST
#     statement, the `else` / `catch` / `rethrow` ones as a LEADING comment on
#     the FIRST.
#   AXIS 2, inline row: MEASURED PER CELL, and only two of the four can red —
#     stated rather than assumed, per Core #12's "if neither form of red is
#     possible, say what it pins instead". Killing a site's anchor
#     (`format_arm_body(body, 0)` — `last_real_content_before(0)` is `None`, so
#     both hooks no-op) moves `inline x case` onto its own line above the next
#     arm, and `inline x else` likewise: an ARM is not a statement, so nothing
#     else claims its comment and it drifts to lead the arm below.
#     `inline x catch` and `inline x rethrow` do NOT red, and the reason is
#     structural: each is the tail of an ordinary `int v = …` STATEMENT that
#     ends on the same line, so `format_block_stmts`' own trailing hook puts the
#     comment back in the identical position. Those two cells therefore pin the
#     OUTPUT (the comment stays on the header line) rather than the anchor —
#     they would still catch a change that moved the comment, just not one that
#     only neutralised this site's anchor. The `do:` row above is what pins the
#     anchor at those two positions, and it reds at all four.
#
# ⚠ NOT a runnable fixture, and no harness treats it as one: the suite-layout
#   matrix PARSES and FORMATS these files and asserts fixpoint + re-parse +
#   idempotence + no trailing whitespace. `rethrow` propagates, so this file is
#   deliberately not expected to execute.

void if_else(int x):
    if x == 1:
        print(1)
    else:    # the fallback branch
        print(2)

void stmt_match_else(int x):
    match x:
        case 1:
            print(1)
        else:    # everything that is not 1
            print(2)

void for_else():
    for i in 0..3:
        print(i)
    else:    # ran to completion
        print(9)

void while_else():
    while false:
        print(1)
    else:    # never entered the loop
        print(2)

String meta_match_else[T]():
    meta match typename(T):
        case "int":
            return "i"
        else:    # every other type
            return "o"

int expr_match_else(int x):
    int r = match x:
        case 1:
            10
        else:    # the expression-position clause
            20
    return r

meta if platform() == "macos":    # the mac branch
    int backend_id():
        return 1
elif platform() == "windows":    # the windows branch
    int backend_id():
        return 2
else:    # every other platform
    int backend_id():
        return 3

int rem_or_throw(int a, int b):
    return a %! b

int arm_body_do_shapes(int x):
    int do_case = match x:
        case 1: do:    # do x case
            int a = 1
            a + 1
        else:
            0
    int do_else = match x:
        case 9:
            9
        else: do:    # do x else
            int b = 2
            b + 1
    int do_catch = rem_or_throw(x, 0)! catch (e): do:    # do x catch
        int c = 3
        c
    int do_rethrow = rem_or_throw(x, 1)! rethrow (String e): do:    # do x rethrow
        int d = 4
        d
    return do_case + do_else + do_catch + do_rethrow

int arm_body_inline_shapes(int x):
    int in_case = match x:
        case 1: 11    # inline x case
        else:
            0
    int in_else = match x:
        case 9:
            9
        else: 12    # inline x else
    int in_catch = rem_or_throw(x, 0)! catch (e): 13    # inline x catch
    int in_rethrow = rem_or_throw(x, 1)! rethrow (String e): 14    # inline x rethrow
    return in_case + in_else + in_catch + in_rethrow

void main():
    if_else(1)
    stmt_match_else(1)
    for_else()
    while_else()
    print(meta_match_else[int]())
    print(expr_match_else(1))
    print(backend_id())
    print(arm_body_do_shapes(1))
    print(arm_body_inline_shapes(1))
